ll work  basically just like on a MacBook with  all of the same gesture support now  alongside these cases are also ones  without track pads so there are some  people who will buy these being unaware  that there are trackpad cases coming  soon that'll be much more useful for  their needs so in this video I'm going  to break down the most popular keyboard  cases into three different categories  ones without track pads one with track  pad  and once created by Apple the keyboard  cases without trackpads are generally  going to be much cheaper than the others  this includes cases like the bridge pro  case for the iPad pro that we've been  using a bunch and we personally love is  currently on sale for $100 on Amazon  which is a pretty great deal and Bridge  also has similar models available for  the other iPads as well like for the  budget 10.2 inch iPad and iPad air for a  hundred and thirty dollars on Amazon  there's also Logitech which provides the  slim folio pro case for the iPad pro  which is quite a bit more rugged and  drop resistant currently on sale for $96  for the iPad pro but do keep in mind  that since this case protects the back  you need to make sure the camera bump  fits your iPad pro model because the  2020 iPad pro square bump is quite a bit  larger than on the 2018 model and of  course Logitech has basically the same  options for the iPad in the iPad air for  around $100 now what might surprise you  is that Logitech has a keyboard case  with the built-in trackpad for 50  dollars more that adds a ton of  functionality with the cursor support  spending that extra $50 will make a  world of a difference for a lot of  people because it basically turned your  iPad into a laptop Bridge also has their  own Pro Plus keyboard coming in just  about a week which also packs a trackpad  but it's only for the iPad pro being  priced at 230 dollars but because of the  aluminum design and the fully adjustable  hinge is more like a laptop than the  logitech option and then there's the  final category keyboard cases made by  Apple and we're also splitting them into  two categories with and without track  paths for the cases without the 10.2  inch iPad in the iPad air get the smart  keyboard which costs a hundred and fifty  nine dollars and the iPad pro gets the  smart Keyboard folio for a hundred and  seventy nine dollars and interestingly  Apple is making a keyboard case with a  trackpad only for the iPad pro and not  for the other iPads and this one in my  opinion is the ultimate keyboard case  I'll explain why in just a minute so now  let's get into which keyboard case you  should buy for each different use case  if you don't really care about  using your iPad like a laptop with  cursive support then you're better off  getting a case without a trackpad since  you'll save quite a bit of cash if  you're in that boat then I'd recommend  going for the logitech slim folio case  since it's got a high-quality rugged and  protective build it has a built in Apple  pencil holder and it's the least  expensive out of the bunch but if you  could see yourself enjoying cursus  support I would 100% recommend spending  the extra cash on one of the logitech  slim combo cases with a built-in  trackpad for either the 10.2 inch iPad  or the iPad air unfortunately Logitech  doesn't have one of these for the iPad  pro chest yet so if you're an iPad pro  user you're left with the choice of  either the bridge Pro Plus for $230 or  apples magic keyboard case for $300 now  I know that's a lot of money for a case  but I'm fully confident that it's gonna  be so good that it'll take the iPad pro  to a whole new level  and there will be people who will regret  choosing a cheaper case instead there's  a few features of the magic keyboard  case that you literally cannot get  anywhere else and this is what makes it  worth it first off is the only trackpad  keyboard case that's made by Apple that  means you're getting very high build  quality and Apple is known for making  the best trackpads  out there there are already reviews of  the bridge pro plus keyboard case that  complain about the trackpad not really  being that great with scrolling speed  being inconsistent and some gestures not  working like they're supposed to so if  you want to get the best trackpad  experience you want the magic keyboard  case and unlike the Pro Plus which uses  Bluetooth to connect the magic case uses  the physical smart connector on the back  of the iPad pro which means it'll never  lose its connection and it doesn't have  a battery built-in which means you never  have to charge it unlike you have to do  with the Pro Plus in a big benefit is  that the lack of a battery and the  magnetized design makes it much slimmer  so it weighs less and is more portable  one of the best and most unique things  about the probe plus is the fully  adjustable hinge but the magic keyboard  case 1ups it by having a totally unique  floating ipad design  which places your iPad pro closer to you  and the hinge is adjustable as well and  then there's the fact that it gets the  same scissor switch keyboard as the 16  inch MacBook Pro so you're getting a  real laptop keyboard unlike Apple smart  keyboard folio which has these pretty  weird feeling keys and the ones on the  bridge pro which feel a bit mushy  another bonus is that it features  backlighting all through the physical  smart connector which also gives you one  feature you can't get on any other  keyboard case and that's passed through  charging the magic keyboard case has a  USB C port built into the side of the  hinge which routes your charging power  through the smart connector to charge up  your iPad pro and this is awesome  because it leaves your main USB C port  free to connect external drives and  devices the only other way to achieve  this is by attaching an expensive USB C  hub that has passed through charging and  extra ports which some people don't want  to deal with so for those reasons I  fully recommend pre-ordering the magic  keyboard case which should be coming out  next month instead of the Bridge Pro  Plus the only reason I would get the Pro  Plus is if you don't want to spend a  dime over 230 dollars for a case or if  you can find it for under $200 if it  goes on sale I do agree that $300 is  still very expensive but if you choose a  third-party case and you start having  various reliability issues like with the  trackpad not working correctly bluetooth  losing its connection and the battery  dying you might start to wish you just  went straight for the magic keyboard  instead so hopefully this video helps  you guys decide which keyboard case you  should buy for your iPad and if it did  we'll have some links down below for  each of these options thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]
 

